---
import OpportunityColour from "../../../components/studies/OpportunityColour.astro";

import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "utility-machinery-2026-11-02");
const ships = [
	["wasp", "Wasp"],
	["scout", "Scout"],
	["fighter", "Fighter"],
	["corvette", "Corvette"],
];
const engines = [
	["ion", "Light ion"],
	["heavy-ion", "Heavy ion"],
	["fusion", "Fusion"],
	["plasma", "Plasma"],
];
const root = "/studies/utility-machinery-2026-11-02/";
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section>
		<details>
			<summary>01 / Machinery with a physical presence</summary>
			<p>
				Beside the Wasp’s raised body and the ion engine’s assembled housing, a
				utility should feel like another manufactured object in the same world.
				Energy storage, solar charging, gravity charging and automatic repair
				offer four different characters: held power, a surface reaching for
				light, a mechanism gathering energy from motion through gravity, and a
				machine tending another machine. These are starting impressions to
				investigate, not settled construction choices.
			</p>
			<p>
				The family needs weight without becoming four identical boxes. The ships
				and engines below establish the visual foundation: a few solid parts,
				clear changes of plane, deliberate joins and dark spaces between
				assemblies. Visible working parts should explain how an object is put
				together. At enlarged scale, a housing should have thickness and a
				mechanism should seem supported; at flight scale, the silhouette and
				largest masses must carry its identity.
			</p>
			<p>
				This cycle explores which proportions, working structures and family
				resemblances make these utilities belong. A small device beside a ship
				must hold its own without overwhelming the ship’s character. The shared
				reference panel stays here throughout the exploration, so each proposal
				can be compared against the same established objects. Capacity, charging
				balance, repair rules and final mounting arrangements remain outside
				this visual inquiry.
			</p>
		</details>
		<div class="reference" id="reference">
			<h3>Production reference / ships and engines</h3>
			<p>
				Static captures of current production artwork, 2 November 2026. Ships
				use default loadouts, nose up; engines use the aft lighting pose,
				exhaust down. Enlargements share a scale within each row: ships 2.5×,
				engines 8×. Every small view uses the same 0.65× flight scale. Compare
				small views at normal browser zoom.
			</p>
			{[ships, engines].map((group) => (
				<div class="grid">
					{group.map(([id, label]) => (
						<figure>
							<figcaption>{label}</figcaption>
							<img
								src={`${root}${id}.png`}
								width="240"
								height="280"
								alt={`${label}, enlarged production artwork`}
							/>
							<div class="small">
								<img
									src={`${root}${id}-flight.png`}
									width="100"
									height="90"
									alt={`${label} at gameplay scale`}
								/>
							</div>
							<span>0.65× / flight scale</span>
						</figure>
					))}
				</div>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>
	<section id="exploration">
		<h2>02 / Construction as family character</h2>
		<details>
			<summary>02.1 / A housing or a supporting cradle</summary>
			<p>
				The Wasp and ion suggest a provisional family principle: substantial
				primary masses, with smaller fittings visibly seated in them. Their
				character comes from shaped volumes before surface detail. The fusion
				engine adds another possibility already present in this world: separated
				masses connected across an open space. This suggests that belonging need
				not require a closed shell.
			</p>
			<p>
				These two storage sketches test where to put the structural weight. Both
				contain the same paired, faceted cells, terminal bridge and lower
				connector; both occupy 22 × 28 drawing units with identical lighting and
				palette. A wraps the cells in deep protective shoulders. B exposes their
				sides and carries them between end blocks, with a recessed central spine
				and lateral retaining clamps. The dark gaps in B are spaces between
				objects, not decorative markings.
			</p>
			<div class="grid">
				<figure>
					<figcaption>Wasp / raised body</figcaption>
					<img
						src={`${root}wasp.png`}
						width="240"
						height="280"
						alt="Production Wasp reference"
					>
					<div class="small">
						<img
							src={`${root}wasp-flight.png`}
							width="100"
							height="90"
							alt="Wasp at flight scale"
						>
					</div>
					<span>Production · 2.5× / 0.65×</span>
				</figure>
				<figure>
					<figcaption>Ion / protective housing</figcaption>
					<img
						src={`${root}ion.png`}
						width="240"
						height="280"
						alt="Production light ion reference"
					>
					<div class="small">
						<img
							src={`${root}ion-flight.png`}
							width="100"
							height="90"
							alt="Light ion at flight scale"
						>
					</div>
					<span>Production · 8× / 0.65×</span>
				</figure>
				{[
					["housing", "A / Armoured cassette"],
					["cradle", "B / Exposed cell cradle"],
				].map(([id, label]) => (
					<figure>
						<figcaption>{label}</figcaption>
						<img
							src={`${root}${id}.svg`}
							width="240"
							height="280"
							alt={`${label}: provisional energy storage construction`}
						/>
						<div class="small">
							<img
								src={`${root}${id}-flight.svg`}
								width="100"
								height="90"
								alt={`${label} at provisional gameplay scale`}
							/>
						</div>
						<span>Sketch · 8× / 0.65×</span>
					</figure>
				))}
			</div>
			<p>
				A tests a compact, guarded character; B tests a serviceable machine
				whose assembly can be understood from outside. The working hypothesis is
				that thick supports around an exposed functional part could unite
				storage cells, solar hinges, gravity machinery and repair joints without
				forcing them into the same silhouette. These sketches only test that
				proposition on storage. They do not establish the other devices, moving
				behaviour, or final equipment size.
			</p>
			<p>
				At small scale, inspect the overall mass and the openings first. If the
				exposed construction disappears there, it may still explain assembly
				when enlarged, but it cannot yet carry the family’s gameplay identity.
				No construction direction has been selected.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href="#reference">Return to all eight production references ↑</a>
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="family-promise">
		<details>
			<summary>02.2 / What does this machinery promise the pilot?</summary>
			<p>
				02.1 succeeded in making the problem visible: the first construction
				sketches felt approachable enough to discuss, but cassette versus cradle
				did not explain why this family matters. Neither construction was
				selected. The next question moves from assembly to character.
			</p>
			<p>
				The ships can be read as angry triangles announcing “I am an attack
				ship”; the engines announce “I burn power.” This family needs an equally
				direct promise. The support-class analogy is useful: a cleric matters
				through the relationship they create with the player, not through the
				category “support.” Here, “I sustain you,” “I gather power for you” and
				“I empower you” describe reasons to care. “Utility” remains a working
				label, not a character brief.
			</p>
			<p>
				Three deliberately provisional postures below explore that relationship.
				Each uses the same central working core, palette, lighting and display
				scale. Surrounding masses change its stance: enclosing, reaching or
				reinforcing. These are visual metaphors for a family, not three proposed
				equipment types. The drawings cannot establish their emotional reading
				without human interpretation.
			</p>
			<div class="promises">
				{[
					[
						"sustain",
						"I keep you going",
						"A steadfast companion",
						"Low shoulders cup the working core; weight sits beneath it. The intended feeling is dependable reserve: something that stays with you through a difficult run. The risk is reading as passive armour or a container.",
					],
					[
						"gather",
						"I turn surroundings into strength",
						"A resourceful companion",
						"The assembly opens outward, with substantial receiving ends and a core between them. The intended feeling is alertness and exchange with the world. The risk is reading as a weapon or a claw rather than a provider.",
					],
					[
						"empower",
						"I help you do more",
						"An enabling companion",
						"Paired raised masses reinforce the core and point in a shared direction. The intended feeling is readiness and added capability. The risk is becoming another engine or implying speed alone.",
					],
				].map(([id, promise, character, description]) => (
					<figure>
						<figcaption>{promise}</figcaption>
						<img
							src={`${root}${id}.svg`}
							width="240"
							height="280"
							alt={`${character}: speculative machinery posture`}
						/>
						<div class="small">
							<img
								src={`${root}${id}-flight.svg`}
								width="100"
								height="90"
								alt={`${character} at provisional flight scale`}
							/>
						</div>
						<h4>{character}</h4>
						<p>{description}</p>
					</figure>
				))}
			</div>
			<h4>One promise, four expressions</h4>
			<p>
				A family promise must survive changes of function. These are possible
				meanings for the existing four roles, not new gameplay commitments.
			</p>
			<div class="meanings">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Role</th>
							<th>Keep you going</th>
							<th>Turn surroundings into strength</th>
							<th>Help you do more</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th>Storage</th>
							<td>Reserve when you need it</td>
							<td>Hold what you have gathered</td>
							<td>Headroom for your next action</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Solar charging</th>
							<td>Replenishment you can rely on</td>
							<td>Find value in available light</td>
							<td>Renew your capacity to act</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Gravity charging</th>
							<td>Recover energy along the journey</td>
							<td>Make the environment an opportunity</td>
							<td>Convert a manoeuvre into possibility</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Automatic repair</th>
							<td>Help you endure damage</td>
							<td>Restore what remains usable</td>
							<td>Return you to action</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				“I help you do more” is a promising umbrella because it puts the pilot’s
				agency first. “I keep you going” may better express care and endurance.
				“I turn surroundings into strength” has a distinctive, resourceful
				attitude, but fits storage and repair less directly. These are
				hypotheses; a mixture or a different promise may describe the family
				better.
			</p>
			<p>
				The useful result of this round is a desired relationship and the
				visible qualities that support it. A chosen phrase would guide another
				drawing round across all four functions; it would not automatically
				approve any posture shown here.
				<a href="#reference"
					>The production reference panel remains the shared visual
					foundation.</a
				>
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="wartime-roles">
		<details>
			<summary>02.3 / Keep going—and make it count</summary>
			<p>
				02.2 succeeded in explaining the role behind the machinery. The
				discussion now centres on “I keep you going” and “I turn surroundings
				into strength”; broad empowerment is no longer the leading direction.
				The support-class analogy remains useful, but the wartime framing gives
				support a harder purpose: endurance can be a means of gaining advantage,
				not simply reassurance.
			</p>
			<p>
				Extra armour, repair, energy gathering and energy storage all help a
				ship remain operational. They also let a pilot make use of something:
				protection, recoverable damage, available energy or a reserve held for
				later. “Exploitation” here means making those resources and
				opportunities count. It need not mean consuming the environment or
				scavenging an enemy. Armour enters this discussion as another example of
				the family’s role; its inclusion does not settle a new equipment design.
			</p>
			<h4>Four possible attitudes</h4>
			<div class="meanings">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Promise</th>
							<th>Relationship with the pilot</th>
							<th>What makes it compelling</th>
							<th>What it could miss</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th>I keep you in the fight</th>
							<td>A steadfast combat companion</td>
							<td>
								Damage and depletion do not immediately take away your ability
								to act. Armour buys tolerance, repair restores condition,
								gathering replenishes and storage holds reserve.
							</td>
							<td>Can sound passive if staying alive is its only ambition.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>I make opportunity usable</th>
							<td>A resourceful accomplice</td>
							<td>
								You notice something worth exploiting and the machinery lets you
								benefit: available light, a gravity encounter, protection or
								stored energy.
							</td>
							<td>
								Not every resource comes from the surroundings; repair and
								armour should not be forced into a harvesting metaphor.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>I help you outlast</th>
							<td>A relentless campaign machine</td>
							<td>
								The family expresses persistence under pressure: staying
								effective after ordinary reserves would have run out.
							</td>
							<td>
								Can imply slow attrition or an opponent who runs out first.
								Neither combat pacing nor opponent resource rules are
								established here.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>I turn reserves into advantage</th>
							<td>A calculating quartermaster</td>
							<td>
								Protection, restored condition and stored energy feel like means
								to pursue an intention. The promise is having enough in hand to
								commit when an opening appears.
							</td>
							<td>
								Can overemphasise hoarding or imply active release controls.
								This is a character hypothesis, not a new control scheme.
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<h4>One situation, different reasons to care</h4>
			<p>
				Imagine a battered ship approaching another dangerous stretch with
				energy running low. This is a fictional design situation, not a claim
				about implemented encounters. “Keep you in the fight” makes the
				machinery feel dependable. “Make opportunity usable” draws attention to
				what the route offers. “Help you outlast” gives continued operation a
				stubborn character. “Turn reserves into advantage” makes the remaining
				margin feel like something the pilot can commit with intent.
			</p>
			<p>
				The hardware might perform the same functions in every reading. What
				changes is the attitude: shelter, resourcefulness, persistence or
				calculated advantage. This is why industrial versus scientific, and
				cassette versus cradle, cannot settle the family’s character on their
				own.
			</p>
			<h4>A working synthesis: resourceful endurance</h4>
			<p>
				<strong
					>“I keep you in the fight by making the most of what you have and what
					you encounter.”</strong
				>
				This combines the two promising directions without making every device a
				collector. Armour preserves your margin; repair restores it; gathering
				adds to it; storage carries it forward. The common purpose is
				maintaining the means to act under pressure.
			</p>
			<p>
				That suggests machinery with a tenacious, purposeful character: valuable
				because it helps the pilot persist and take opportunities. “Combat
				support,” “sustainment” and “survival machinery” are possible working
				names, each with a different emphasis; none is selected. The
				exploitation angle contributes resourcefulness and intent, while “keep
				you going” supplies the family’s centre. Whether that should feel more
				like a loyal companion or a calculating war machine remains open.
			</p>
			<p>
				This round deliberately uses roles and situations rather than new
				drawings. It establishes no visual selection, combat mechanic, balance
				rule or additional control. The next visual investigation can express
				the relationship that emerges from this discussion. The earlier sketches
				and production references remain available for that work.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="family-decision">
		<details>
			<summary>02.3 / Direction established: “I create opportunities.”</summary>
			<p>
				<strong>The family’s promise is “I create opportunities.”</strong>
				This advances the discussion beyond keeping the ship operational or
				exploiting opportunities that already exist. These machines create room
				for the pilot to act.
			</p>
			<p>
				Armour can create room to take a risk. Repair can create another chance
				after damage. Gathering energy can make an otherwise unavailable action
				possible; storage can preserve that possibility until the pilot needs
				it. These are interpretations of the agreed promise, to investigate
				through design—not additional mechanics or settled balance.
			</p>
			<p>
				Endurance and resourcefulness remain useful means, but opportunity
				creation is the family’s centre. The earlier endurance-led synthesis is
				superseded. No silhouette, construction or working name has been
				selected. The next visual question is how machinery can communicate that
				it makes an opening possible, while remaining a substantial object
				alongside the ships and engines.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="family-identity">
		<details>
			<summary>02.4 / An identity that survives a small silhouette</summary>
			<div class="decision">
				<h4>Established: a clear accent identifies the family</h4>
				<p>
					A distinct, vivid family colour carried by selected physical parts is
					a useful identity cue. Bases, connections or other recurring parts can
					carry it, much as dark engine bodies and their coloured exhaust ends
					identify engines. This is an accepted direction for family
					recognition, alongside other qualities still to be discovered.
				</p>
				<p>
					The exact hue, its placement and the additional family cues remain
					open. Amber is a successful example, not a selected colour; neither
					the open yoke nor the transverse bridge is selected as the
					construction. The family promise remains “I create opportunities.”
				</p>
			</div>
			<p>
				<strong
					>“I create opportunities” remains the family promise. Clean, crisp and
					energetic is the colour intent.</strong
				>
				A minimal palette can carry vivid colour on substantial parts; minimal
				does not mean muted. The next hypothesis is a bright, repeated
				structural feature that identifies the family while the working parts
				distinguish its members.
			</p>
			<p>
				Two proposals use the same four experimental bodies and the same vivid
				amber palette. A gives each device an open, three-sided yoke: a physical
				opening around the mechanism, suggesting available room or possibility.
				B gives each device a broad transverse power bridge: a strongly
				connected assembly, suggesting capability made available. These meanings
				are hypotheses, not symbols a player can be assumed to understand.
			</p>
			{[
				[
					"gate",
					"A / Open yoke",
					"A bright opening carried by two raised shoulders and a deep connecting heel.",
				],
				[
					"band",
					"B / Power bridge",
					"A bright transverse mass connects the working assembly, interrupted by a raised central fitting.",
				],
			].map(([motif, title, description]) => (
				<div class="identity-round">
					<h4>{title}</h4>
					<p>{description}</p>
					<div class="grid">
						{[
							["storage", "Storage"],
							["solar", "Solar charging"],
							["gravity", "Gravity charging"],
							["repair", "Repair"],
						].map(([kind, label]) => (
							<figure>
								<figcaption>{label}</figcaption>
								<img
									src={`${root}identity-${motif}-${kind}-color.svg`}
									width="240"
									height="250"
									alt={`${label} with ${title}, enlarged faceted construction`}
								/>
								<div class="small">
									<img
										src={`${root}identity-${motif}-${kind}-color-flight.svg`}
										width="100"
										height="70"
										alt={`${label} with ${title} at 0.65 scale`}
									/>
									<img
										src={`${root}identity-${motif}-${kind}-mono-flight.svg`}
										width="100"
										height="70"
										alt={`${label} with ${title}, colour removed at 0.65 scale`}
									/>
								</div>
								<span>6× enlarged · 0.65× colour · 0.65× greyscale</span>
							</figure>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			))}
			<p>
				The small views are approximately 17–23 pixels across at normal browser
				zoom. Greyscale copies help expose whether shape contributes identity
				when hue is unavailable. Each coloured part has a light-facing plane, a
				broad saturated face and a dark side; the colour belongs to an assembled
				object rather than a painted icon. The solar wings, gravity rotor and
				repair jaws are provisional visual fixtures, not selected mechanisms.
			</p>
			<h4>Beside established equipment</h4>
			<div class="identity-strip">
				{["ion", "heavy-ion", "fusion", "plasma"].map((id) => (
					<img
						src={`${root}${id}-flight.png`}
						width="100"
						height="90"
						alt={`${id}, production flight scale`}
					/>
				))}<img
					src={`${root}identity-gate-storage-color-flight.svg`}
					width="100"
					height="70"
					alt="Open-yoke storage proposal at flight scale"
				><img
					src={`${root}identity-band-storage-color-flight.svg`}
					width="100"
					height="70"
					alt="Power-bridge storage proposal at flight scale"
				>
			</div>
			<p>
				Amber is energetic, but the production plasma engine already uses an
				orange accent, so hue alone cannot establish a distinct family. The
				proposed transverse or open construction must do additional work. Future
				guns are expected to be long and pointed; their artwork is not
				established here, so separation from guns remains a later comparison.
				The full
				<a href="#reference">ship and engine reference panel</a>
				remains available above.
			</p>
			<p>
				This round can establish a promising combination of shape and colour, or
				reveal that neither cue carries enough identity. It cannot prove that a
				tiny object literally communicates “opportunity,” nor settle the colour
				for the entire game. Family recognition and the feeling of available
				capability are related but separate judgments.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="working-character">
		<details>
			<summary>02.5 / Different ways of creating an opportunity</summary>
			<p>
				<strong
					>The family is connected by colour on its structure, attachments and
					connections. It need not share a silhouette.</strong
				>
				The remaining body palette—white, grey or black—is unresolved. These
				sketches retain a cool grey body and vivid amber structure to focus this
				round on behaviour, without selecting those colours.
			</p>
			<p>
				The next hypothesis is that each device becomes most itself when
				working. Storage holds potential quietly. Solar enlarges its collecting
				surface. Gravity reaches beyond the object through a field. Repair
				distributes its work through four drones. Their different shapes and
				behaviours can belong together because the accent identifies what holds,
				joins and attaches the machinery.
			</p>
			<p>
				The animated trial informed the discussion; the accepted delivery uses
				only the resting drawings preserved in 02.6. Working states are outside
				this delivery.
			</p>
			<p>
				These are illustrative resting and working states, independent of the
				game loop. The solar proposal uses hinged collectors; projected solar
				fields remain an alternative. Gravity ellipses explore an FX layer
				without defining force or range. Repair drones orbit a schematic service
				area without a ship or damage targets; their eventual routes and scale
				relative to a hull remain open. Storage is deliberately identical in
				both columns.
			</p>
			<p>
				The comparison keeps geometry and camera fixed between states, uses no
				randomness, and runs a 1.3-second solar deployment, a 2.8-second field
				cycle and a 6-second drone loop. These timings serve inspection only. On
				narrow phones the enlarged drawings fit the columns; the 0.65× views
				retain their size. Reduced-motion mode preserves deployed poses without
				continuous movement.
			</p>
			<p>
				The useful discovery is whether these distinct working behaviours give
				each member a compelling role while the accent on physical structure
				holds the family together. Mechanisms, body palette and animation are
				still proposals.
				<a href="#reference"
					>Production ships and engines remain the visual foundation.</a
				>
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="family-colour">
		<details>
			<summary>02.6 / Accepted colour and resting parts</summary>
			<p>
				The accepted reference uses RGB 206, 219, 23 (#CEDB17) on the existing
				connector, strut and attachment geometry. Two production Wasps carry
				Light ions, with storage and solar on the first and gravity and repair
				on the second. All devices rest above the wings in this reference.
			</p>
			<OpportunityColour />
			<p>
				The given resting parts and simple colour shading are accepted for this
				delivery. Individual part refinement can follow in separate studies. No
				deployment, drones in flight, pulsing fields or other animations are
				included.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="readiness">
		<h2>03 / Readiness: a static family can be delivered</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted conclusion:</strong>
			the family creates opportunities. Its identity uses #CEDB17 on connectors,
			struts and attachments; its members may have different silhouettes. The
			four given resting drawings in 02.6 are the exact delivery reference,
			including their current body colours and simple accent shading.
			Working-state ideas remain useful future research, but this delivery
			contains no animations.
		</p>
		<p>
			The reference is already inspectable as static SVG equipment over
			production Wasp artwork. Its four assets are resting-storage.svg,
			resting-solar.svg, resting-gravity.svg and resting-repair.svg in the study
			component directory. The reference uses a 0.35 equipment scale, production
			wing-mount centres, top-of-hull layering and a 0.65 flight-scale check.
			These are the preserved comparison conditions; production layering must
			follow each hull’s mounting rules.
		</p>
		<p>
			Production drawBuild already handles mounts, orientation, hull layering
			and engine artwork. Its non-engine fitting renderer currently draws
			generic casings and a battery mark, so these new faces still require
			integration. Battery already has a part contract and capacity effect.
			Solar charging, gravity charging and repair are not defined as those
			equipment types in the current loadout contract; displaying their art does
			not implement their game rules.
		</p>
		<p>
			Integration provides artwork only. Gravity Charger remains conceptual:
			this scope adds no device ID, loadout entry or gameplay rules. Static
			artwork integration is feasible with existing procedural drawing systems
			and no added dependencies. A shared accent definition and reusable part
			faces should serve both the Lab and the renderer. Equipment contracts must
			be defined with Zod before any new IDs or data shape are added. Mechanics
			for the three new functions are separate work, not a prerequisite for
			preserving or displaying the accepted artwork.
		</p>
		<p>
			Remaining verification concerns are readability at actual mount size,
			clipping and layering on other hulls, and distinction from other bright
			game colours. Human acceptance here settles the illustrated family and
			resting parts; it does not claim an implemented gameplay release.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section id="scope">
		<h2>04 / Scope: resting artwork first</h2>
		<p>
			Separate study-quality review:
			<code>docs/reviews/utility-machinery-2026-11-02.md</code>.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Study accepted, 2 November 2026.</strong>
			The selected colour is RGB 206, 219, 23 (#CEDB17). Deliver the four given
			resting parts with that family accent. No animations or working-state FX
			are included. No change to docs/design.md is proposed: the feature’s
			identity belongs in its equipment contract and shared drawing definitions.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Shared static artwork — small.</strong>
				Extract the accepted resting faces and accent shading into reusable
				procedural part definitions, retaining 02.6 as the fidelity reference.
				Document the family promise, accent placement and resting-only scope in
				the ship-builder specification. Verify all four at enlarged and gameplay
				scales in the Lab.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Shared artwork integration — medium, follows slice 1.</strong>
				Make all four artworks available in shared drawing definitions and the
				Lab. Connect storage to the existing battery renderer. Gravity charging,
				solar charging and repair remain conceptual artworks wherever no
				corresponding device exists: add no device IDs, catalog or loadout
				entries, simulation behaviour or invented rules. Verify mount transforms
				and layering with labelled visual fixtures.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Individual device work — separate studies.</strong>
				Solar, gravity and repair need their own behaviour contracts before
				becoming functional equipment. Future shape refinement or activation
				design belongs in those studies; the accepted static family provides the
				starting point. Their scope and size remain undecided.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The first two slices can deliver and verify the visual result
			independently of new game mechanics. Acceptance closes this design study;
			production implementation, publishing and separate studies have not been
			started by this closure.
		</p>
		<p>
			Verification for implementation should include contract, type, unit and
			build checks plus browser inspection of the shared art on actual hulls.
			Animation is explicitly excluded. The study’s earlier checks establish
			availability of the examples, not production integration.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
